/-!
# Universal injectivity into a flat module and ideal quotients

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0AS5, `lemma-check-universally-injective-into-flat`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flat-target-ideal-quotient-injectivity-test).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w t

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace LinearMap

/-- Universal injectivity into a flat module is detected modulo finitely generated ideals.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0AS5, `lemma-check-universally-injective-into-flat`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flat-target-ideal-quotient-injectivity-test).

Proof:
1. Identify `(R/J) ⊗ X` with `X/JX` by sending `a ⊗ x` to `a • x` modulo `JX`.
   Its inverse sends the class of `x` to `1 ⊗ x`; bilinearity makes this vanish on `JX`.
   These maps intertwine `1 ⊗ v` and the induced quotient map. Tensor symmetry gives the
   same statement for `v ⊗ 1`. Thus universal injectivity implies the quotient tests.
2. Extend the tests to an arbitrary ideal `I`. If `v(x)` belongs to `IM`, write it as a
   finite sum `∑ a_j • m_j`, with `a_j ∈ I`. The ideal `J` generated by these coefficients
   is finitely generated and contained in `I`, and `v(x) ∈ JM`. The hypothesis gives
   `x ∈ JL ⊆ IL`. Hence the quotient map modulo `I` is injective as well.
3. For a finitely presented test module `Q`, choose finite generators and filter it by
   their successive spans `Q_i`. The successive quotient is `R/I_i`, where `I_i` consists
   of the scalars taking the new generator into `Q_{i-1}`. Step 2 makes `1 ⊗ v` injective
   on these cyclic quotients, even though `I_i` need not be finitely generated.
4. Apply `lem-weil-pbc-tensor-right-exact` to each sequence
   `Q_{i-1} → Q_i → R/I_i → 0`, tensored with `L` and with `M`. Flatness of `M`,
   as in `def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map`, makes the first arrow in the bottom row injective.
   The squares involving `1 ⊗ v` commute on pure tensors, hence on all tensors by
   `def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product`.
5. Induct through the filtration, starting with the zero tensor product. If an element
   `z ∈ Q_i ⊗ L` maps to zero, Step 3 kills its image in `(R/I_i) ⊗ L`. Right exactness
   lifts it to `y ∈ Q_{i-1} ⊗ L`. Commutativity and injectivity of the bottom inclusion
   imply `(1 ⊗ v)(y) = 0`; induction gives `y = 0`, hence `z = 0`.
6. Apply the first, arbitrary-ring equivalence of `lem-trf-universal-injective-finite-test`
   to pass from finitely presented test modules to every test module. Tensor symmetry
   returns exactly the maps `v ⊗ 1_Q` in the statement.
-/
theorem flat_target_ideal_quotient_injectivity_test
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    {L : Type v} [AddCommGroup L] [Module R L]
    {M : Type w} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [Module.Flat R M]
    (v : L →ₗ[R] M) :
    (∀ (Q : Type (max u v w t)) [AddCommGroup Q] [Module R Q],
      Function.Injective (v.rTensor Q)) ↔
    ∀ (J : Ideal R), J.FG →
      Function.Injective
        ((J • (⊤ : Submodule R L)).mapQ (J • (⊤ : Submodule R M)) v
          (Submodule.smul_top_le_comap_smul_top J v)) := by
  sorry

end LinearMap
